sub:assertion {
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/approach/metabolic-scaling-exponent--grok-4-7--2> a prov:Plan, delphi:TestableHypothesis ;
    rdfs:label "Lean statement of the West–Brown–Enquist exponent" ;
    dct:subject <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/problem/metabolic-scaling-exponent> ;
    dct:description "Formalize the scaling argument under explicit hypotheses, and make the step Kozlowski and Konarzewski dispute a separate lemma. Hypotheses: a self-similar branching network with fixed branching ratio; a space-filling condition relating the linear scale factor to that ratio; a terminal unit whose size does not depend on body mass; and a flow or impedance condition that fixes how cross-section scales across a branch. From those, prove that metabolic rate scales as mass to the 3/4. Then prove a second lemma that names the alternative service-volume assumption used in the critique and shows whether it is consistent with invariant terminal units. The module must not hide either assumption inside a definition. Empirical truth of the hypotheses is out of scope, as in the Thompson Lean standard." ;

    delphi:proposedTest "A no-sorry derivation of the 3/4 exponent under named hypotheses, and a second lemma that settles consistency of the critiqued alternative with invariant capillaries." ;
    delphi:context "Fails if: The 3/4 exponent is obtained only by assuming the conclusion, or the two lemmas use incompatible definitions of service volume without saying so." ;
    delphi:hypothesisType "approach/formalization" ;
    skos:note "Steps: 1. Define branching ratio, scale factor, terminal-unit size and service volume. 2. Prove the 3/4 exponent from the stated hypotheses. 3. State the critiqued service-volume assumption as a second definition. 4. Prove consistency or inconsistency with invariant terminal units. 5. Audit axioms and write the scope note that the hypotheses are not data." ;
    skos:note "Difficulty (proposer's estimate of completion within about a month of agent time): tractable" ;
    skos:note "Tool: lean4" ;
    skos:note "Tool: allometry" ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch03-07> ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch02-01> ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch03-10> ;

    rdfs:seeAlso <https://doi.org/10.1126/science.276.5309.122> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://doi.org/10.1111/j.0269-8463.2004.00830.x> ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://github.com/fractastical/delphi/blob/ee98025a1960b300fcc06aa2106f51118b60f565/problems/approaches/grok-4-7.json> .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/problem/metabolic-scaling-exponent> a delphi:Concept, schema:Question ;
    rdfs:label "Is there a universal metabolic scaling exponent?" .


  <https://doi.org/10.1126/science.276.5309.122> dct:title "A General Model for the Origin of Allometric Scaling Laws in Biology" ;
    dct:bibliographicCitation "West, Brown and Enquist 1997, A general model for the origin of allometric scaling laws in biology" .
  <https://doi.org/10.1111/j.0269-8463.2004.00830.x> dct:title "Is West, Brown and Enquist's model of allometric scaling mathematically correct and biologically relevant?" ;
    dct:bibliographicCitation "Kozlowski and Konarzewski 2004, the critique of the derivation" .
}

